# OS-aware script launcher

`run.sh` reads the adjacent `.man` contracts under `bin`, `dev`, and `sys`; it
does not maintain a second script list. Run it from a checkout or stream it
without installing this repository.

> **Behavior:** The launcher detects Linux, macOS, or Windows internally,
> hides incompatible and non-launchable commands, passes arguments unchanged,
> and never uses `eval`. Streamed runs use a temporary source archive and
> remove it when the selected command exits.

The launcher uses Python 3.11 or newer. If it is unavailable, an existing `uv`
can supply Python. `curl` and `tar` are also needed for streamed runs.

## Usage

```sh
./run.sh --help
./run.sh list
./run.sh matrix
./run.sh man disk
./run.sh security-audit audit --scope system
./run.sh disk health
./run.sh clean
./run.sh --self-test
```

With no arguments, the responsive dashboard opens when `uv` and a terminal are
available; otherwise the compatible script list is printed.

Use a listed short name or its repository-relative path. Remaining arguments
are passed unchanged to that command. Ambiguous names, traversal, malformed
manual metadata, symlinks, unsupported platforms, and incompatible scripts are
rejected.

`./run.sh matrix` publishes the support and privilege contract directly from
the same manual metadata. “Supported” means the command implements that OS;
the command's manual still lists required native tools. Windows streaming uses
Git Bash with native Windows Python or `uv`; WSL is treated as Linux. “Mixed”
privilege means normal-user operation with elevation only for named repair or
system actions. Slurm submission uses the configured scheduler account rather
than operating-system administrator access. The apply-gate column describes
the shared destructive-action gate; a dash does not promise read-only behavior,
so consult the manual for direct report, cache, or catalog writes.

`man SCRIPT` renders the adjacent manual with Glow when installed, or prints
plain Markdown. Manuals for other operating systems remain viewable.

`./run.sh --self-test` validates all three operating-system catalogs without
running their commands.

## Run without installing

GitHub `/blob/` pages are HTML and cannot be piped to a shell. Use the raw
endpoint:

```sh
RUN='https://github.com/keys-i/scripts/raw/refs/heads/main/run.sh'

curl -fsSL "$RUN" | bash
curl -fsSL "$RUN" | bash -s -- list
curl -fsSL "$RUN" | bash -s -- matrix
curl -fsSL "$RUN" | bash -s -- man disk
curl -fsSL "$RUN" | bash -s -- disk health
```

Manuals also remain directly readable from their raw source:

```sh
curl -fsSL \
  https://raw.githubusercontent.com/keys-i/scripts/main/bin/disk.man |
  glow -
```

## Exit status

`list`, `matrix`, help, and the self-test return `0`; launcher validation errors
return `2`; and a missing Python interpreter returns `127`. A launched command
replaces the launcher, so its exit status passes through unchanged.
